{"skill":{"slug":"browserwing","displayName":"browserwing"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1770084672878,"changelog":"Initial release of BrowserWing – browser automation via HTTP API.\n\n- Control page navigation, element interaction (click, type, select), and advanced operations like screenshots and JavaScript execution.\n- Extract data and analyze accessibility snapshots for structured, stable element references.\n- Supports multiple element identifier types: RefID, CSS selectors, XPath, and text.\n- Batch operations and wait-for-element functionality included for robust scripting.\n- API base URL is fully configurable via the BROWSERWING_EXECUTOR_URL environment variable.","changelogSource":"auto","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":18752,"sha256":"3f158aaac42357e1ba9645865ef55f307ba508da4dde4bbb56808bed951132f7","contentType":null},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2084,"sha256":"6f853ac1f921719f7c03b1e4673442bf1f2dbc9e151e4fb88f79c9d43a4bedd8","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789030054865,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"6dcecd0e64a5f90f5619a7cdae1bbee9f150d4c6f8954e05eec731352d27907f","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/6dcecd0e64a5f90f5619a7cdae1bbee9f150d4c6f8954e05eec731352d27907f","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: browserwing\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill bundle describes a browser automation tool with several high-risk capabilities. Specifically, the `file-upload` endpoint allows the agent to upload arbitrary files from the host machine to web forms, posing a significant data exfiltration risk. Additionally, the `evaluate` endpoint enables arbitrary JavaScript execution within the browser context, which could be leveraged for client-side attacks or data exfiltration from browsed pages. While these capabilities are plausible for browser automation, their power and potential for misuse, combined with the configurable `BROWSERWING_EXECUTOR_URL` (allowing interaction with external endpoints), classify this as suspicious due to the broad permissions and risky capabilities without clear malicious intent in the skill's own instructions.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779161007736},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":20,"severity":"LOW","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":3,"checkedAt":1789030054865},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This skill is a browser-automation helper with expected but powerful access that can type credentials, inspect pages, run JavaScript, capture screenshots, and view network activity without enough safety guidance.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Using a configurable BrowserWing executor URL is coherent for this purpose, and the default is localhost, but a remote executor URL would receive browsing commands and potentially sensitive page data.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The artifact is a single Markdown skill with no bundled executable code or dependencies inspected; its frontmatter metadata appears malformed, which is an install/metadata quality issue rather than direct malicious behavior.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"Instructions repeatedly encourage broad use of the executor, including operating on the currently active browser tab and using batch operations, but do not add clear limits, redaction guidance, or confirmation requirements for credentials and sensitive sites.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"No system persistence or privilege escalation is present, but the skill can use an existing browser session and automate authenticated actions, so users should treat it as high-impact browser-account authority.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated purpose matches browser automation, but the documented capabilities include login automation, form filling, screenshots, page HTML/text extraction, JavaScript execution, file upload, and network/console inspection, which can expose sensitive page contents or account access.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Install only if you trust the BrowserWing executor you will connect to. Avoid using it on banking, admin, medical, workplace, or other sensitive sites unless you explicitly intend to expose that page content and any typed credentials to the executor. Prefer a local executor, review the target tab before actions, and do not provide passwords or secrets through this skill unless necessary.","findings":"[SQP-2] expected: The scanner concern is supported in part: page extraction, screenshots, JavaScript execution, console inspection, and network request capture are expected browser-automation features, but the artifact lacks meaningful privacy and data-handling warnings.\n[E1] expected: The cited curl command targets the configured executor API, defaulting to localhost; this is expected integration behavior, not artifact-backed exfiltration by itself.\n[SQP-2] expected: The login automation example explicitly types a username and password, which is purpose-aligned but under-disclosed because the skill gives no credential-handling warning or user-confirmation guidance.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789030054865}}}}}